window.FIGURES = [{"id":"1.1","chapter":1,"title":"Demand for sand and gravel and its extraction from rivers in northeast Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 1.1. Demand for sand and gravel and its extraction from rivers in northeast Bangladesh. (a) Estimated annual global demand for sand and gravel, 2000 to 2024, derived from cement production; redrawn from values digitized from Park et al. (2026, Fig. 1a) under CC BY 4.0. (b) Domestic extraction of non-metallic construction minerals and gross domestic product (GDP) of Bangladesh, 1970 to 2023; redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 1), using data from the International Resource Panel Global Material Flows Database and Statista. Open circles mark 2014 and 2015, for which the extraction record appears incomplete. (c) Manual extraction of sand into boats on the Sari River at Lalakhal, Sylhet. (d) Stone-processing yards at Bholaganj, Sylhet, along the road to the Indian border.","paragraph":263,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-1-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1310,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-1-1.jpg","sha256":"47a10460c7d45862fdad06c47bef9b428cfeb39cb0d80375b7cb1ce9eea5a3c4"}]},{"id":"1.2","chapter":1,"title":"The Driver-to-Management Pathway for Sustainable Sand and Gravel Mining (DMP-SGM) and its application in the current thesis","caption":"Figure 1.2. The Driver-to-Management Pathway for Sustainable Sand and Gravel Mining (DMP-SGM) and its application in the current thesis. (a) The four stages of the pathway and the chapters of this thesis that address each stage. (b) Conceptual example of a single river, in which demand (Driver 1) stimulates extraction at spatially distinct sites (Extent 1 and 2) whose impacts propagate upstream and downstream beyond the mined reaches. (c) Separation in space, and the resulting scale mismatch, between drivers, extent and impacts. (d) The scale mismatch investigated in this thesis. Panels (b) and (c) are reproduced from Park et al. (2026, Fig. 2) under CC BY 4.0; panel (a) is redrawn and adapted from the same figure; panel (d) is original to this thesis.","paragraph":269,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-2-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1747,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-2-1.jpg","sha256":"85fd86d6278be59000e72abb5d44e8b1e195fa32abff0b2cf6be774a3addda71"}]},{"id":"1.3","chapter":1,"title":"Movement of sand from source rivers to urban markets in Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 1.3. Movement of sand from source rivers to urban markets in Bangladesh. (a) Distribution of sand extracted from the Someshwari River to districts of Bangladesh, with Dhaka as the primary destination and distribution hub; reproduced from Yasmin et al. (2024, Fig. 4) under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. (b) Sand carried from boats to a roadside storage and sales yard at Sharighat, Sylhet. (c) A boat loaded with sand near Sharighat.","paragraph":275,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-3-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1287,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-3-1.jpg","sha256":"f9981852c7c6cc2407977c70c1061b68f10712f407b41871211568ca9d69ef37"}]},{"id":"1.4","chapter":1,"title":"The extent and character of mining in the Ganges–Brahmaputra–Meghna basin and northeast Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 1.4. The extent and character of mining in the Ganges–Brahmaputra–Meghna basin and northeast Bangladesh. (a) Sand mining sites in the Ganges–Brahmaputra–Meghna catchment, classified as dry or wet mining and as measured or not measured; reproduced from Daham et al. (2024, Fig. 1) under CC BY 4.0. (b) Water-filled pits left by stone and gravel extraction on the floodplain at Bisnakandi, Sylhet. (c) Boats used for artisanal sand extraction on the Rakti River, Sunamganj.","paragraph":287,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-4-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1390,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-4-1.jpg","sha256":"96717f2956259f0815f8afda08ec99c5cf7e4c6ee3293d14421fe9234e4e88d9"}]},{"id":"1.5","chapter":1,"title":"Mining vessels and mining-sculpted bedforms on the Mekong River at the Chaktomuk junction, Phnom Penh, Cambodia","caption":"Figure 1.5. Mining vessels and mining-sculpted bedforms on the Mekong River at the Chaktomuk junction, Phnom Penh, Cambodia. (A) Location in the Mekong basin. (B) Number of mining vessels detected in PlanetScope imagery, 2016 to 2022, and their density over the survey area. (C, D) Multibeam bathymetry in September 2013 and May 2022. (E, F) Bed profiles along the transects shown in (C) and (D), comparing 2013 and 2022. Reproduced from Hackney et al. (2025, Fig. 1) under CC BY 4.0.","paragraph":297,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-5-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1772,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-5-1.jpg","sha256":"84f82a5044888bcf30c18750431e799cf232217ee00906f754a4a26d407431c2"}]},{"id":"1.6","chapter":1,"title":"River–aquifer interactions (a) before and (b) after in-channel mining, and (c) the effect of riverbed incision on salinity intrusion, illustrated for the Mekong Delta","caption":"Figure 1.6. River–aquifer interactions (a) before and (b) after in-channel mining, and (c) the effect of riverbed incision on salinity intrusion, illustrated for the Mekong Delta. Incision lowers both the bed and the water surface, which steepens the gradient from aquifer to river and leads to long-term groundwater decline and reduced recharge. Panel (c) is adapted from Binh et al. (2021). Reproduced from Park et al. (2026, Fig. 8) under CC BY 4.0.","paragraph":302,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-6-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1121,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-6-1.jpg","sha256":"8b66df39209d1101c7b53b1acaca4a472b973ed7b10700a0b7bec92ac96012a0"}]},{"id":"1.7","chapter":1,"title":"Scales of sand and gravel mining impacts and the position of the study rivers","caption":"Figure 1.7. Scales of sand and gravel mining impacts and the position of the study rivers. (a) Relative severity and magnitude of key impacts from artisanal to mechanized extraction; bar thickness indicates the magnitude and spatial scale of each impact. (b) Spatial and temporal envelopes of impacts along the river continuum, from gravel-mining source reaches to sand-mining transfer and sink reaches. Panels (a) and (b) are reproduced from Park et al. (2026, Fig. 11) under CC BY 4.0. The upper and lower bands, added here, position the study rivers of this thesis by mode of extraction and by bed material.","paragraph":309,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-7-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1897,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-7-1.jpg","sha256":"e0c420517c5cefd004859851c8ac5bbbce2660d00e400c354ec3e3b18d483412"}]},{"id":"1.8","chapter":1,"title":"Pathways from artisanal and mechanized (industrial) sand and gravel mining to primary and secondary environmental and socio-ecological impacts","caption":"Figure 1.8. Pathways from artisanal and mechanized (industrial) sand and gravel mining to primary and secondary environmental and socio-ecological impacts. The width of each link reflects how often, or how strongly, that pathway is reported in the studies reviewed by Park et al. (2026). Reproduced from Park et al. (2026, Fig. 13) under CC BY 4.0.","paragraph":316,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-8-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":721,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-8-1.jpg","sha256":"878f192610b7e8f3a19123df8900d5750ec04315a54f3ece54221bfa06fb772f"}]},{"id":"1.9","chapter":1,"title":"Structure of the thesis","caption":"Figure 1.9. Structure of the thesis. Research chapters are positioned by the DMP-SGM stages they address (columns) and by spatial scale and bed material (rows). Chapters 1 to 3 provide the framework, setting and methods for all research chapters, and Chapter 9 synthesizes their findings across the pathway.","paragraph":346,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-1-9-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-1-9-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-1-9-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":944,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-1-9-1.jpg","sha256":"12bfd978c4302947432d4a239e4f98cf4290ae3a8c425552ab64efeef6195060"}]},{"id":"2.1","chapter":2,"title":"The study region: rivers draining the southern margin of the Shillong Plateau (Meghalaya, India) into northeast Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 2.1. The study region: rivers draining the southern margin of the Shillong Plateau (Meghalaya, India) into northeast Bangladesh. (a) Study rivers are colored by the material extracted from them (gravel, sand, or both on the Piyain River), from the plateau to downstream of their gauges; circles mark the principal extraction sites, colored by the material extracted; triangles and squares mark the Bangladesh Water Development Board water-level gauges and groundwater wells used in Section 2.2 and Chapters 6 and 7. The inset shows the location of the map. (b) The study rivers from west to east: principal site, material and mode of extraction, upstream catchment area at the gauge (at Bholaganj for the Dhalai River), most of it in Meghalaya (HydroRIVERS), the Bangladesh Water Development Board records used, and the chapters that analyze each river. Relief: hillshade of the Copernicus GLO-90 digital surface model (European Space Agency, 2026), with vertical exaggeration of 2 and an elevation tint that fades to white below c. 60 m. Rivers: HydroRIVERS (Lehner & Grill, 2013), reaches draining more than 150 km². Faults: GEM Global Active Faults Database (Styron & Pagani, 2020). Boundaries and towns: Natural Earth (n.d.), 1:10 million; the international border is generalized at this scale. Gauges and wells: Bangladesh Water Development Board station coordinates; Bholaganj: the Sada Pathor site in the Chapter 4 site inventory.","paragraph":360,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-2-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-2-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch2_01.png","width":2160,"height":1665,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch2_01.png","sha256":"8cf8184ad71bf7d1eb1850de5b818ae4566ad6f98f3b768a2501e4014ebbbbeb"}]},{"id":"2.2","chapter":2,"title":"Schematic north–south profile from the Shillong Plateau to the Sylhet Depression, not to scale","caption":"Figure 2.2. Schematic north–south profile from the Shillong Plateau to the Sylhet Depression, not to scale. (a) The plateau, an uplifted block of Archean and Proterozoic basement with a Mesozoic–Miocene cover on its southern flank, is thrust southward along the Dauki Fault over the subsiding Sylhet Trough; the dip of the fault at depth is uncertain. Rivers leave the plateau across the border onto alluvial fans and a sand-bed plain that drains to the haors of the Sylhet Depression and the Meghna River basin. The southwest monsoon produces orographic rainfall over the plateau. (b) Schematic of the downstream change in bed material, the material extracted in each zone, and the study sites where each is extracted. Sources: Bilham and England (2001); Johnson and Alam (1991); FAP 6 (1993); R. Ahmed (n.d.); S. Q. Chowdhury (n.d.); Sarker (2015); Debu (2025b); field interviews (January 2023).","paragraph":370,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-2-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-2-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch2_02.png","width":2160,"height":1590,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch2_02.png","sha256":"0f638c817ecc9b57c2d99f461f3be0d428f6905d5c84ce7a947e48441d9df724"}]},{"id":"2.3","chapter":2,"title":"Monthly rainfall and discharge regime of four gauged study rivers in the first ten years of each record: (a) Lubha River, (b) Sari-Gowain River, (c) Piyain River and (d) Jadukata River","caption":"Figure 2.3. Monthly rainfall and discharge regime of four gauged study rivers in the first ten years of each record: (a) Lubha River, (b) Sari-Gowain River, (c) Piyain River and (d) Jadukata River. Bars show mean monthly rainfall at the nearest rainfall station; lines indicate the median of measured discharges in each month (log scale). Shading marks the dry season, November to April, when bars are exposed and most sediment extraction takes place. Station names and years are given above each panel. Discharge on the Lubha River is not shown because its record begins in 2009, close to the onset of extraction in c. 2012 (Chapter 5). Data: Bangladesh Water Development Board.","paragraph":381,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-2-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-2-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch2_03.png","width":2160,"height":1470,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch2_03.png","sha256":"238c62ae347e322d866431c805825d11d67705a52794f4205414e833ce62a83d"}]},{"id":"2.4","chapter":2,"title":"Extraction and its setting at the study rivers, from west to east","caption":"Figure 2.4. Extraction and its setting at the study rivers, from west to east. (a) Floating dredgers and sieving towers on the Someshwari River near Durgapur, Netrakona. (b) Sand boats on the Jadukata River where it leaves the hills at Barikka Tila, Sunamganj. (c) Gravel and sand deposited on cropland at Rojinagandha, Sunamganj, by the flash flood of 2021. (d) A stone-crushing plant at Bholaganj on the Dhalai River, with the Shillong Plateau behind. (e) Sand boats and stockpiles on the Piyain River at Jaflong, Sylhet. (f) Sand extracted by hand and with small pumps from the bed of the Sari River, Jaintiapur, Sylhet. (g) A gravel-screening hopper and stockpiles on the floodplain of the Lubha River at Lubhachorra, Sylhet.","paragraph":396,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-2-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-2-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch2_04.jpeg","width":2160,"height":894,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch2_04.jpeg","sha256":"0be8cf09a5a748d03fbbf605fde9db4a8294e9805ba8ddcc5f6033192c783537"}]},{"id":"2.5","chapter":2,"title":"Extraction and its regulation at the study sites, 2008 to 2025","caption":"Figure 2.5. Extraction and its regulation at the study sites, 2008 to 2025. (a) Phases of extraction at Lubhachorra, Jaflong, Bholaganj and Durgapur. The dotted line marks the Balumahal and Soil Management Act, 2010, and the dashed line the change of government on 5 August 2024; the shaded band is enlarged in (b). (b) Legal and governance events, 2020 to 2025, under the sand regime (above the axis) and the gravel regime (below). In (a), cross-hatching marks extraction without authorization: gravel extraction at Jaflong and Bholaganj after August 2024, and sand extraction on the Someshwari River after leasing was suspended on 13 March 2024. Sources: Chyon et al. (2026); Chapter 6; Chakrobortay (2021); D. Chowdhury (2022, 2025); Ashish-Ur-Rahman and Chakraborty (2023); Hossain (2023); The Daily Star (2024, 2025); Debu (2025a, 2025b); Deshwara (2017); The Business Standard (2025); J. Islam (2026).","paragraph":419,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-2-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-2-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch2_05.png","width":2160,"height":1980,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch2_05.png","sha256":"c3a4d67291d6f9c666c47d88e52b8a7966137fd30c1c95dc01c832a961b7f18d"}]},{"id":"3.1","chapter":3,"title":"Research design of the present thesis","caption":"Figure 3.1. Research design of the present thesis. Rows are the four types of evidence used, while columns depict the DMP-SGM stages they inform (Figure 1.2); each box provides an analysis and the chapters that use it. Arrows mark the main links between chapters. The onset dates of Chapter 4 are compared with national drivers and governance events (Hypothesis 1) and supply the timing of extraction used in Chapters 6 and 7, while the physical results of Chapter 6 frame the analysis of governance in Chapter 8. Chapter 9 compares the rivers across all stages.","paragraph":430,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-3-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-3-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch3_01.png","width":2100,"height":1896,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch3_01.png","sha256":"246981170092ed951d91889cea16b7dbae1cc90301c0a498c6a91c40106f245c"}]},{"id":"3.2","chapter":3,"title":"Periods covered by the imagery, fieldwork and monitoring records, shown by river","caption":"Figure 3.2. Periods covered by the imagery, fieldwork and monitoring records, shown by river. Solid bars mark records used in the analyses of this thesis. Open bars with dotted outlines mark records that are available but used, at most, to describe the baseline in Chapter 2, and the near-river wells that failed the screening of Chapter 7. Split bars show water level (upper) and discharge (lower) at the same gauge, and points in the cross-section rows mark the years of repeat surveys. Fieldwork points mark the reconnaissance visits of January 2021 and January 2023 and the social research of January 2024 (Someshwari) and February 2024 (Lubha). Vertical shaded bars mark the dated phases of extraction on the Lubha (Chapter 5) and Someshwari (Chapter 6) rivers. Dashed lines mark the ban on mining on the Lubha River, enforced from February 2021, and the halting of gravel extraction at Jaflong in February 2020 (Section 2.5.2). Phases of extraction are not shown for the other rivers, whose onsets are dated from imagery in Chapter 4. The GWref bar shows the period paired with GWn in Chapter 6; the raw GWref record begins in 1975. Data: Bangladesh Water Development Board; Bangladesh Meteorological Department.","paragraph":450,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-3-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-3-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch3_02.png","width":2100,"height":2191,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch3_02.png","sha256":"3ecedcf513e67353cc896322d11c707a942f9183329740da5aedfafed8989008"}]},{"id":"3.3","chapter":3,"title":"Interpreting the extent of sediment extraction from imagery","caption":"Figure 3.3. Interpreting the extent of sediment extraction from imagery. (a) Each pixel of an idealized reach is labeled water or exposed sediment from a spectral index, with vegetation masked. (b) Change between two dates is interpreted from pixels that switch class; each transition has two interpretations, one geomorphic and one hydrological. (c) Cross-section through an idealized bar between fixed banks at stages h₀ and h₁; the exposed width (arrows) narrows as water rises. (d) The same section at h₀ after the bar is lowered, with the surface before extraction dashed. The exposed width equals that of (c) at h₁, and thus a single image cannot separate higher water from a lowered bar. (e) Control 1, used where dry-season stage varies little between years: images are retained only where stage on the image date falls within a narrow band (filled points), and the rest are excluded (open points). (f) Control 2, used where water stage varies more: exposed area is plotted against stage on the image date and compared with a relation fitted to images from before extraction. Case (c) lies on the relation, whereas case (d) plots below it by ΔA. ΔA records reduced exposure at a given stage, not its cause, which could also be erosion, channel shift or a change in sediment supply, and not the volume removed. Diagram is schematic.","paragraph":468,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-3-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-3-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch3_03.png","width":2100,"height":2040,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch3_03.png","sha256":"1b7bf3df7be06e72da28a6aa75154c77d2a0a49001472ce1e1973a4733a0f947"}]},{"id":"3.4","chapter":3,"title":"Comparisons that narrow the explanations for a fall in river stage or groundwater level, matching Table 3.4 and generalizing the framework developed on the Someshwari River (Figure 6.2) to the four rivers of Chapter 7","caption":"Figure 3.4. Comparisons that narrow the explanations for a fall in river stage or groundwater level, matching Table 3.4 and generalizing the framework developed on the Someshwari River (Figure 6.2) to the four rivers of Chapter 7. Each panel shows the two outcomes a comparison can give, drawn with equal weight; under each outcome, ‘Inference’ names the explanations that remain consistent with it, and the last line states what the comparison cannot separate. (a) Stage against rainfall and discharge. (b) Water level at the same discharge: a fall largest at low flow against an equal fall at all flows. (c) How a fall accumulated: gradually against an abrupt, lasting step in the daily record. (d) The fall at the gauge nearest extraction against that at a gauge farther downstream. (e) A near-river well against a reference well. (f) Timing against dated extraction in the gauged reach. The outcomes observed on each river, and the explanations that remain after the sequence, are reported in Chapters 6 and 7. Diagram is schematic.","paragraph":474,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-3-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-3-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch3_04.png","width":2100,"height":1800,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch3_04.png","sha256":"f76b4642e705b3f7a8f5b56d56a8bed1a4fb56491437fc810adfcee3b877aee3"}]},{"id":"3.5","chapter":3,"title":"Social research on the Lubha and Someshwari rivers: what was collected, how respondents were selected, how the material was analyzed and in which chapters the data are used","caption":"Figure 3.5. Social research on the Lubha and Someshwari rivers: what was collected, how respondents were selected, how the material was analyzed and in which chapters the data are used. In the Someshwari River respondents row, bar widths are proportional to the number of respondents in each occupational group. All Someshwari River components were collected during the January 2024 visit; individual session dates were not recorded. BELA: Bangladesh Environmental Lawyers Association.","paragraph":483,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-3-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-3-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch3_05.png","width":2100,"height":1326,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch3_05.png","sha256":"17e8754781ada78b50a9c57f384098f62e3723e0dbca505dd704f1d838095a74"}]},{"id":"4.1","chapter":4,"title":"Scene classification at two reaches, with December Landsat 5 and Sentinel-2 scenes classified into sand, water and vegetation by a supervised classifier (classification and regression tree)","caption":"Figure 4.1. Scene classification at two reaches, with December Landsat 5 and Sentinel-2 scenes classified into sand, water and vegetation by a supervised classifier (classification and regression tree). (a) The Dhalai River at Bholaganj in 1990, 2000, 2010 and 2020, with the 1990 and 2020 false-color composites. Pits pockmark the bed by 2010, and the channel is largely water by 2020, with sand delivered by the monsoon visible at the upstream end (arrow in the 2020 classification). (b) The Piyain River at Jaflong over the same four decades. Water appears inside the bars by 2000 and fills the channel bend by 2010.","paragraph":524,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_01.png","width":1297,"height":1765,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_01.png","sha256":"a6c6bb0cab719d52972b11b6b50eb0293c5141a7b3d7cd7a4e1e44cd26f167ce"}]},{"id":"4.2","chapter":4,"title":"The January low-flow signal","caption":"Figure 4.2. The January low-flow signal. (a) Schematic of the result classes of Table 4.3. For each class, the panel shows the January scene before and after the change inside a fixed site outline (dashed red line), the sign of change in the areas of water, sand, vegetation and bare ground, and the time series of area that the change produces through the 38 Januaries, with the onset of extraction marked. The first three columns show extraction-type classes. Floodplain pits follow the pit pattern on a floodplain outline, and depots and fills share one column. The class is assigned from the sign of change, together with the first-conversion map and sub-meter imagery. The schematic extends Figure 3.3. (b) Results of the classification at five sites, one per class, in the January Landsat scenes before and after the change. Each row shows the scene in color (red and green bands, without the blue band), the same scene as a shortwave-infrared, near-infrared and red composite, in which water is dark, dry sand is pale pink and vegetation is green, and the three classes inside the site outline (red lines). From the top, the rows show the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura (E060, pit extraction, 2010 and 2024), the Dharla River floodplain at Patgram (E013, floodplain pits, 1996 and 2010, pit-field mode), the Someshwari River at Durgapur (N05, pit extraction, 2015 and 2024), the Teesta River near Dimla (N17, bar vegetation, 2005 and 2020) and the Jamalpur Economic Zone (N28, land filling, 2016 and 2019, pit-field mode). White patches in the 2024 Durgapur scene are cloud. Scale bar 1 km.","paragraph":532,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_02.png","width":2861,"height":2050,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_02.png","sha256":"8cbf8c4ce5fddc4d910edeff223716d82a535905bbf84a1ede164fd064eb2b31"},{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-2-2.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-2-2.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_03.png","width":1617,"height":1502,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_03.png","sha256":"95520b7f70b1086a247c41f7de1eee909566feaa544c69804d0b3f0c790c8e42"}]},{"id":"4.3","chapter":4,"title":"Result class at the 337 analyzed sites","caption":"Figure 4.3. Result class at the 337 analyzed sites. (a) The whole study area on a hillshade, with HydroRIVERS reaches of mean discharge above 150 m³ s⁻¹, the sector bands S1 to S6 (Table 4.1) and the outlines of panels b to g. In total, 51 sites lie in India or Bhutan. The triangle marks the Sevoke Gorge, where the Teesta River leaves the Himalayan foothills. (b) to (g) Satellite images of the six reaches that contain most of the extraction-type sites, each 67 by 51 km: (b) the Tentulia salient, (c) the Teesta River, shown for contrast, with bar vegetation and no extraction-type signal, (d) the Old Brahmaputra and Bhogai rivers, (e) the Someshwari and Kangsa rivers, (f) the Jadukata and Dhopajan-Chalti rivers, and (g) the Sari-Gowain, Piyain and Lubha rivers with Jaflong. Site codes are shown for the extraction-type classes, places named in the text are labeled in yellow (the Karatoya River in b, the Chanpur Chora fan in f and Bisnakandi in g), and each panel gives its class counts and a 10 km scale bar. Each class is shown by both marker shape and color. Hillshade: Esri, USGS, NASA; imagery: Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics; rivers: HydroRIVERS v1.0; boundaries: Natural Earth.","paragraph":550,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_04.png","width":2100,"height":1770,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_04.png","sha256":"857dd24f5cd341cde721dbdb0cb704f363756cf87e034f8998788dae999a6a24"},{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-3-2.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-3-2.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-4-3-2.jpg","width":1500,"height":1693,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-4-3-2.jpg","sha256":"7367af7121a83c6eaacba93b0b6a15f251281d51e10a2b6d7161f9ac2d98d426"}]},{"id":"4.4","chapter":4,"title":"Result classes by sector at the 179 sites with a persistent change","caption":"Figure 4.4. Result classes by sector at the 179 sites with a persistent change. Each bar gives the share of each class among the sites of that sector with a persistent change, and the number in a segment is its number of sites (segments of one site are not numbered). The column at the right gives the number of sites with a persistent change and the number of all sites in the sector. The 158 sites with no signal are not shown. Sectors are as in Table 4.1 and classes as in Table 4.3, and every site is listed in Table 4A.2.","paragraph":552,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_06.png","width":1827,"height":1017,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_06.png","sha256":"c54a89f065ac2aaf9d561374b65c0c57b0cf988dfc0bcb3d01c68a49a8e73f42"}]},{"id":"4.5","chapter":4,"title":"Onset and spread of extraction","caption":"Figure 4.5. Onset and spread of extraction. (a) First year of change at each dated site, plotted against longitude from west to east along the border, by result class. Horizontal bars are the sector medians for the extraction-type classes (pit extraction, floodplain pits, possible dredging and bar loss), and their values are given under the sector labels. Dashed lines mark the Sand and Soil Management Act of 2010, the 2014 court order on the Sylhet extraction sites and the 2020 cancellation of the Sylhet leases. (b) Cumulative number of sites with a detected extraction-type change by dry season, with one panel per sector, and cumulative number of the 21 dated depot and land-filling sites of all sectors (last panel). Dashed lines mark the same three events.","paragraph":557,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_07.png","width":1756,"height":1201,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_07.png","sha256":"6ba697554da127aaad06b51ad1143a8ae496e3f57a37139839a13b6167f1a691"}]},{"id":"4.6","chapter":4,"title":"Extraction footprint at the 79 extraction-type sites","caption":"Figure 4.6. Extraction footprint at the 79 extraction-type sites. (a) Conservative footprint by class, 1988 to 2025, stacked. Each site’s series is a three-season running median of usable January scenes, with its 1988 to 1995 level subtracted. The dashed line is the upper bound, which adds the decrease in the area of bar sand. (b) Mean footprint by sector for 1988 to 2000, 2001 to 2010, 2011 to 2015, 2016 to 2020 and 2021 to 2025. (c) The twelve largest site footprints in 2021 to 2025, colored by class. (d) Number of water-filled pits of at least three Landsat pixels at the 14 pit-extraction and 16 floodplain-pit sites. Vertical gray lines in (a) and (d) mark the 2010 Act, the 2014 Sylhet court order and the 2020 cancellation of the Sylhet leases.","paragraph":567,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_08.png","width":3115,"height":2012,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_08.png","sha256":"e4379b85cae6ec1fb69c695fb2a243d4aae54175d33d837e238ec024bbfff761"}]},{"id":"4.7","chapter":4,"title":"Yards and sand-filled land in January Landsat true-color images for six seasons from 1996 to 2024, with bare ground (NDVI < 0.25 and not water) shown in yellow","caption":"Figure 4.7. Yards and sand-filled land in January Landsat true-color images for six seasons from 1996 to 2024, with bare ground (NDVI < 0.25 and not water) shown in yellow. (a) The Burimari land port yards (N84). (b) The Osmani airport expansion, Sylhet (N88). (c) The Sylhet Hi-Tech Park (N21). (d) The Jamalpur Economic Zone (N28). The label at the left of each row gives the onset. A year in the corner of an image marks the nearest usable season where the named season was hazy or partial, and white patches are cloud. Scale bar 1 km.","paragraph":576,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_09.png","width":1617,"height":1160,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_09.png","sha256":"234bcc39e9849ef47f22c34c181a64dc9257308b96fea30308bcf66bbfef4811"}]},{"id":"4.8","chapter":4,"title":"Bare ground at the depots and sand fills of the study area compared with the extraction footprint, and the growth of sand-filled land east of Dhaka","caption":"Figure 4.8. Bare ground at the depots and sand fills of the study area compared with the extraction footprint, and the growth of sand-filled land east of Dhaka. (a) Net bare ground above the 1988 to 1995 level at the 15 depot and stockpile sites and the 13 sand-filled blocks, counted from the visual onset of each site and stacked. The dashed line is the cumulative peak area of the 21 dated sites, with each site added in the January of its onset. The solid line is the conservative extraction footprint of the 79 extraction-type sites (Figure 4.6a). All series are three-season running medians of usable January scenes. (b) Cumulative sand-filled land in east Dhaka, 2001 to 2020, from the classification of Islam (2023). Dashed lines mark the 2010 Act, the 2014 Sylhet court order and the 2020 lease cancellation. (c) The east Dhaka fill (orange) mapped for 2001 to 2020 on the road and river network (Islam, 2023; basemap Esri, HERE, Garmin).","paragraph":579,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_10.png","width":1756,"height":1281,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_10.png","sha256":"9f8804a1e2a0398bef27ac7535ddf7db4872272ccc7c45d8220398bfbc0dbfa8"}]},{"id":"4.9","chapter":4,"title":"Four site histories from the January Landsat time series: (a) the Jadukata River fan at Laurergarh (N01, S5), (b) the Dhalai River at Bholaganj (N04, S6), (c) Shah Arfin Tila (N69, S6) and (d) the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura (E060, S5)","caption":"Figure 4.9. Four site histories from the January Landsat time series: (a) the Jadukata River fan at Laurergarh (N01, S5), (b) the Dhalai River at Bholaganj (N04, S6), (c) Shah Arfin Tila (N69, S6) and (d) the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura (E060, S5). The left of each row shows true-color January scenes for four seasons, with the site outline in red and low-flow water inside it in blue. Where the whole window was analyzed (pit-field mode), bare ground is shaded. The right of each row shows the areas of water and of sand or bare ground, and the area of sand in 1988 to 1995 that is now water, inside the outline from 1988 to 2025, for usable seasons only. Scale bar 1 km.","paragraph":585,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-9-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-9-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_11.png","width":1635,"height":1907,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_11.png","sha256":"6549c2f723aa6480db697da854a7c0de0c82a91636d4ea677729de692ede7d37"}]},{"id":"4.10","chapter":4,"title":"PlanetScope January mosaics, 2017 to 2026, cropped to the site window, at four sites: (a) the Jadukata River fan at Laurergarh (N01, S5), (b) the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura (E060, S5), (c) the Someshwari River at Durgapur (N05, S4) and (d) the Dhalai River at Bholaganj (N04, S6)","caption":"Figure 4.10. PlanetScope January mosaics, 2017 to 2026, cropped to the site window, at four sites: (a) the Jadukata River fan at Laurergarh (N01, S5), (b) the Dhopajan-Chalti River at Dolura (E060, S5), (c) the Someshwari River at Durgapur (N05, S4) and (d) the Dhalai River at Bholaganj (N04, S6). The mosaics are global monthly basemaps at 4.8 m (Planet Labs PBC, 2026). The bar in the first image of each row is 1 km.","paragraph":588,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-10-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-10-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-4-10-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":822,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-4-10-1.jpg","sha256":"f9c7531678b8a7c19e405a9f87daf4401782608daca6a486746f6bf844ab01c7"}]},{"id":"4.11","chapter":4,"title":"Extraction and recovery at Jaflong on the Piyain River (S6)","caption":"Figure 4.11. Extraction and recovery at Jaflong on the Piyain River (S6). (a) Sub-meter images of the upstream and downstream bends (outline) in December 2003, December 2018, December 2021 and March 2024. In 2003, the bends have alternate and point bars with no large excavations. By 2018, extraction extends along the whole upstream bend. By 2021, the bars have been removed and the deepened downstream bend is full of water, while the upstream bend begins to recover after extraction stopped in 2020. In 2024, the upstream bend has partly recovered and the downstream bend is still mined. Adapted from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S6), with the original panel text removed. (b) PlanetScope January mosaics, 2017 to 2026, over the upstream bend (window E076; Planet Labs PBC, 2026). (c) Area of January Landsat water (solid lines) and sand (dashed lines) inside the upstream (E076) and downstream (E077) windows, 1988 to 2025, for usable seasons. Vertical dashed lines mark extensive extraction by c. 2008, the national ban of 2020 and the resumption of extraction in August 2024.","paragraph":592,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-11-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-11-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_13.png","width":1734,"height":1623,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_13.png","sha256":"ce78257f4f98fda30781d6a7c3e830e364f6007c72ffd963cbb7d2860003a1c4"}]},{"id":"4.12","chapter":4,"title":"Extraction sites of the January time series as seen during fieldwork in January 2023","caption":"Figure 4.12. Extraction sites of the January time series as seen during fieldwork in January 2023. (a) Former gravel pits standing as ponds on the Bisnakandi floodplain (N34), 6 January. (b) Boats and hand-dug pits across the Jadukata fan at Laurergarh (N01), 10 January. (c) A hand-dug pit on the fan holding low-flow water, the surface that the January scene records as water. (d) Sand boats in the low-flow channel between mined bars on the fan. (e) Crusher and stockpile yards at Bholaganj (N23), 9 January. (f) Dredger floats, sieving towers and water-filled pits on the bar of the Someshwari River at Durgapur (N05), 26 January.","paragraph":595,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-12-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-12-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-4-12-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1701,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-4-12-1.jpg","sha256":"4618a8e455df02c29441a5f61f7e7004cbda696a077b00a5fd6e3a87a5db37c3"}]},{"id":"4.13","chapter":4,"title":"The site record plotted against national demand and governance events","caption":"Figure 4.13. The site record plotted against national demand and governance events. (a) Cement production in Bangladesh, 1988 to 2022 (Andrew, 2019; USGS data to 2022), and the conservative extraction footprint of the 79 extraction-type sites (Figure 4.6a), each indexed to 2010 = 100. (b) Onsets of new extraction-type sites per dry season (cumulative totals by sector in Figure 4.5b). Dashed lines mark the 2010 Act, the 2014 Sylhet court order, the 2020 cancellation of the Sylhet leases and the resumption of extraction in August 2024. Dotted lines mark the Patgram closure of 2016, the Dolura suspension of 2018 and the Lubha ban of 2021. (c) Imports of pebbles, gravel and crushed stone (HS 2517) into Bangladesh from India and Bhutan, 2014 to 2024, as reported by the exporting countries (United Nations, 2026). Bhutan did not report 2014, 2015, 2021 and 2024. (d) The extraction footprint plotted against cement production, 1995 to 2022, on logarithmic axes, with points shaded from light to dark by period.","paragraph":601,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-4-13-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-4-13-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch4_15.png","width":2674,"height":2094,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch4_15.png","sha256":"66fefda3c5a0ee32e1f97a2738320b9bc58df2de99767a18c0f28696d1932135"}]},{"id":"5.1","chapter":5,"title":"Location of Lubhachorra, northeast Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 5.1. Location of Lubhachorra, northeast Bangladesh. The primary study area is outlined in white, with the water-level gauge (SW326) and the direction of flow. The inset locates the study area, Jaflong and the Jaintia Hills on the Shillong Plateau. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 2).","paragraph":630,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-1-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1060,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-1-1.jpg","sha256":"98387fe774a2d5b5b0bb3ae703dacec96836e31202fa1acb5fe7ccb00d50a542"}]},{"id":"5.2","chapter":5,"title":"Topography of the study area from the FathomDEM digital elevation model (Uhe et al., 2025)","caption":"Figure 5.2. Topography of the study area from the FathomDEM digital elevation model (Uhe et al., 2025). Where the river leaves the Jaintia Hills, its slope falls from c. 5° to a floodplain gradient of c. 0.4°. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S1).","paragraph":633,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch5_02.png","width":2400,"height":1571,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch5_02.png","sha256":"eb63070992af3ef3c897ecd97410b4e11062ccdb33f06758f89314f339bbab7a"}]},{"id":"5.3","chapter":5,"title":"Water level at the Lubhachorra gauge (SW326)","caption":"Figure 5.3. Water level at the Lubhachorra gauge (SW326). (a) Weekly mean water level, 1990 to 2023, with gaps in the record shaded and the stage on the dates of the dry-season images (Table 5.1). (b) Mean water level by month, with the dry season shaded: peak levels occur from July to September and low, stable levels from December to March. Data: Bangladesh Water Development Board. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S2).","paragraph":636,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch5_03.png","width":2539,"height":1023,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch5_03.png","sha256":"2ae8feee90b2d5ef63caa85a9108c48baa62c927e476e4f02c54dd92ad024b67"}]},{"id":"5.4","chapter":5,"title":"Workflow for estimating (A) gravel extraction in each dry season, (B) monsoon infill of pits, which gives the rate of sediment transport, and (C) the number of migrant workers housed in temporary barracks","caption":"Figure 5.4. Workflow for estimating (A) gravel extraction in each dry season, (B) monsoon infill of pits, which gives the rate of sediment transport, and (C) the number of migrant workers housed in temporary barracks. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S3).","paragraph":641,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch5_04.png","width":2600,"height":2240,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch5_04.png","sha256":"b04b95af03f590ed28daa679438d7394bc5de7a2892a14d5dfb1d2f212e8d361"}]},{"id":"5.5","chapter":5,"title":"Progressive shift from in-channel to floodplain gravel extraction and subsequent partial channel recovery at Lubhachorra, 1972 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.5. Progressive shift from in-channel to floodplain gravel extraction and subsequent partial channel recovery at Lubhachorra, 1972 to 2024. (a) Pre-extraction landscape in 1972: the channel and adjacent abandoned channels/floodplain are largely undisturbed, with natural gravel bars, agricultural land and dense vegetation. (b) By 2008, agricultural land use had intensified, but signs of extraction were absent. (c) The onset of extraction is visible in 2012, concentrated within the channel. (d) By 2015, extraction had expanded downstream in the channel, and large excavation scars in the abandoned channels/floodplain mark the beginning of the shift from channel to floodplain extraction. (e) Extensive, mechanized extraction from the abandoned channels/floodplain continued in 2021. (f) In 2024, the active channel shows substantial recovery after the ban, with infilled pits and reformed bars; vegetation has re-established on parts of the abandoned channels/floodplain. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 3).","paragraph":657,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-5-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1125,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-5-1.jpg","sha256":"cf448ae099788cda541ae61d7b16b1b37c3c83360bd9e324ffb2c1f1bd90ce8e"}]},{"id":"5.6","chapter":5,"title":"Spatial distribution of gravel extraction at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.6. Spatial distribution of gravel extraction at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024. (a, b) In 2012 and 2015, extraction consisted mainly of small artisanal pits along the active channel. (e, f) Over the same period, extraction from the abandoned channels/floodplain developed slowly. (g) Large-scale mechanized mining greatly increased extraction from the abandoned channels/floodplain by 2021. (c, d) After the ban took effect in 2021, the active channel recovered as floodwaters deposited sediment that filled the pits. (h) The abandoned channels/floodplain recovered less, because the deeper pits lie above typical flood levels and remained largely unaffected by floodwaters. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 4).","paragraph":663,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch5_06.png","width":3000,"height":2121,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch5_06.png","sha256":"4591a5ca37f3271c5b277a178b6976c4bb71e8ea907701c1fa21790e4ff822d6"}]},{"id":"5.7","chapter":5,"title":"Gravel extraction in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2005 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.7. Gravel extraction in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2005 to 2024. (a) Area of pits mapped on each dry-season image. (b) Volume extracted each year: the increase in pit volume during the dry season plus the monsoon infill that was mined again (Section 5.3.1); no extraction was mapped after the ban. Shading marks the four periods of extraction, the dashed line the enforcement of the ban in February 2021, and dotted segments the years 2009 to 2011, for which no imagery was analyzed. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 5) with the same data.","paragraph":668,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch5_07.png","width":2247,"height":1813,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch5_07.png","sha256":"4a2e48c1ea1ff4dd7290af9ccc25e8c8317321454d6de2cb5a666ac4296ae719"}]},{"id":"5.8","chapter":5,"title":"Distributions of pit area in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.8. Distributions of pit area in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024. Step histograms show the pits of each zone as a percentage of all pits mapped that year, in logarithmic bins; curves are kernel density estimates of log₁₀ pit area in the same units. The dotted line marks the 300 m² threshold used to assign pit depth, and n gives the number of pits in each zone. Small channel pits dominate in 2012 to 2015, after which both distributions shift toward larger pits. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 6) with the same data.","paragraph":671,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-8-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1359,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-8-1.jpg","sha256":"5797eeacd7f8a367d1270b9477aabca1240083de2c5c7f9ad3007e51a31fb756"}]},{"id":"5.9","chapter":5,"title":"Pit size in (a) the active channel and (b) the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.9. Pit size in (a) the active channel and (b) the abandoned channels/floodplain at Lubhachorra, 2012 to 2024. Median channel pit size increased from 2012 to 2015, with a wider spread and more outliers as channel extraction intensified; pits in the abandoned channels/floodplain grew modestly until 2015 and more after 2015. Boxes show the median and interquartile range (IQR); whiskers extend to 7 × IQR for channel pits and 5.5 × IQR for floodplain pits, and values beyond are plotted individually. Numbers above the boxes give the number of pits; the dashed line marks the ban. The ordinate is logarithmic. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 7) with the same data.","paragraph":675,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-9-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-9-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-9-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1084,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-9-1.jpg","sha256":"bdf212f6dfb0dbf8f67f5a0b1284dddd452538e7450adb506f3e822b25102789"}]},{"id":"5.10","chapter":5,"title":"The active channel at Lubhachorra on (a) 7 January 2019 and (b) 26 March 2024","caption":"Figure 5.10. The active channel at Lubhachorra on (a) 7 January 2019 and (b) 26 March 2024. Over five years, the mining pits visible in (a) were infilled by sedimentation, and gravel dunes formed (b). Images: Maxar, via Google Earth. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S4).","paragraph":677,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-10-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-10-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-10-1.jpg","width":613,"height":281,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-10-1.jpg","sha256":"f51341ba9151383fbf5e30029643bf1ef2efec42748958f727522c07a3417840"}]},{"id":"5.11","chapter":5,"title":"Floodplain degradation, abandoned infrastructure and channel recovery at Lubhachorra and Jaflong","caption":"Figure 5.11. Floodplain degradation, abandoned infrastructure and channel recovery at Lubhachorra and Jaflong. (a) Aerial view of the floodplain on the outside of the meander bend at Lubhachorra, January 2023, with extensive pits left by extraction. (b) Office of a gravel extraction company at Lubhachorra, January 2023, closed after the ban of 2021, with piles of unsold gravel outside. (c) View upstream along Lubhachorra, January 2023: the channel in the background has recovered its natural morphology, and abandoned, rusting gravel-sorting machinery stands on the floodplain. (d) The river valley at Jaflong, March 2015, with large-scale mining of gravel in the upper part of the channel. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. S5).","paragraph":679,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-11-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-11-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-11-1.jpg","width":1430,"height":1430,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-11-1.jpg","sha256":"2dd8950ec2384f9fb0ca8670a8631aa9f59b0ae1cbbc764a97b033c919038624"}]},{"id":"5.12","chapter":5,"title":"Worker barracks associated with gravel mining near Lubha village, 2014 to 2021","caption":"Figure 5.12. Worker barracks associated with gravel mining near Lubha village, 2014 to 2021. (a) 3 March 2014: the floodplain is largely cropland, with barracks built within the channel. (b) 2 March 2017: barracks proliferate in both the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain. (c) 26 December 2020: large-scale geomorphic change in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain, with many barracks housing migrant laborers; the inset shows barracks and piles of extracted gravel around a pit. (d) 2 February 2021: all barracks removed, leaving many pits on the abandoned channels/floodplain, while the active channel shows some recovery in its morphology. Images: Maxar, via Google Earth. From Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 8).","paragraph":689,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-12-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-12-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-12-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1186,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-12-1.jpg","sha256":"9f0b47321408601a7b85dfbec460928878bfdd444962a1e95617f9598d57eb40"}]},{"id":"5.13","chapter":5,"title":"Barracks and migrant workers at Lubhachorra, 2011 to 2024","caption":"Figure 5.13. Barracks and migrant workers at Lubhachorra, 2011 to 2024. (a) Barracks counted on each image in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain; no image was analyzed for 2018. (b) Estimated migrant workers (barracks × 20) and the permanent population of Lubha village in the 2011 and 2021 censuses (Bangladesh Bureau of Statistics, 2013, 2024). Shading marks the periods of extraction, and the dashed line the enforcement of the ban in February 2021. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 9) from the barrack counts.","paragraph":692,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-13-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-13-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-13-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1083,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-13-1.jpg","sha256":"31ef179fbdb2b237f77e6af389b1ec07a68a9f02ee1757f1853ad59bf2aac1a8"}]},{"id":"5.14","chapter":5,"title":"Timeline of the principal changes to the physical and human landscapes of Lubhachorra before, during and after gravel mining","caption":"Figure 5.14. Timeline of the principal changes to the physical and human landscapes of Lubhachorra before, during and after gravel mining. The rows show, from top, i) satellite images of each period, ii) the four periods of extraction, iii) the volume extracted each year in the active channel and the abandoned channels/floodplain, with estimated migrant workers and the dates of the BELA environmental impact assessment (2015), the court decision to ban mining (2020) and enforcement of the ban (February 2021), iv) a summary of each period, and v) field photographs and imagery. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (2026, Fig. 10), with the schematic curves of the published figure replaced by the measured values of Figures 5.7 and 5.13.","paragraph":705,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-5-14-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-5-14-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/thesis-figure-5-14-1.jpg","width":1500,"height":1298,"origin":"Embedded thesis figure","sourceFile":"thesis-figure-5-14-1.jpg","sha256":"28bdbca4eb10807a134c4b9a54fead1b3d275469b45c3e8f8334c099ae08645c"}]},{"id":"6.1","chapter":6,"title":"Location of the study reach and its monitoring network on the Someshwari River, Netrakona District, Bangladesh","caption":"Figure 6.1. Location of the study reach and its monitoring network on the Someshwari River, Netrakona District, Bangladesh. (a) Location of the study area (arrowed). (b) The study reach, with the upstream water-level and discharge gauge (WLu), the downstream water-level gauge in the mined reach (WLd), the near-river groundwater well (GWn), the rainfall station (RF1) and the repeat cross-sections (XS1 to XS4). The reference groundwater well (GWref) lies c. 12 km to the west, beyond the map. (c) The upstream reach in 2024, with no evidence of sand mining. (d) The mined reach in 2024, with extensive excavation and water-filled pits. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 1).","paragraph":742,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_01.png","width":1467,"height":2047,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_01.png","sha256":"f3e9d11542f7fedc71f2c3be759c7fa338b7265ef14cafab86e92b5ebdf60de3"}]},{"id":"6.2","chapter":6,"title":"Analytical framework of the Someshwari River analysis and its three components","caption":"Figure 6.2. Analytical framework of the Someshwari River analysis and its three components. Component 1 detects and quantifies sand extraction from imagery. Component 2 measures the physical response of the bed, river stage, aquifer and floodplain. Component 3 tests rainfall, discharge and regional groundwater as alternative explanations. All outputs of components 1 and 2 are interpreted against the mining periods, and the tests of component 3 identify which explanations remain, following the sequence generalized to other rivers in Section 3.4.2, Table 3.4 and Figure 3.4. Redrawn from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 2).","paragraph":747,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_02.png","width":2720,"height":2479,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_02.png","sha256":"6670e6c89e0dae5922b27b4bc1782ba76e0667112696b55974e4f6ff410e8bd6"}]},{"id":"6.3","chapter":6,"title":"Sensitivity of exposed-bar area to the spatial resolution of imagery, for the 65 mechanized-period dry-season Sentinel-2 scenes classified at their native 10 m and again at 30 m after pixel-mean aggregation of bands 3 and 11","caption":"Figure 6.3. Sensitivity of exposed-bar area to the spatial resolution of imagery, for the 65 mechanized-period dry-season Sentinel-2 scenes classified at their native 10 m and again at 30 m after pixel-mean aggregation of bands 3 and 11. (a) Exposed-bar area, 2017 to 2025, at 10 m (orange) and 30 m (blue). (b) Bar area of each scene at 30 m against 10 m. The dashed line is 1:1 (r² = 0.993, n = 65). The 30 m area is on average 1.15% lower (median 0.98% lower; 95th percentile of the absolute difference 4.19%) and lower in 50 of the 65 scenes. (c) Pixels whose class changes between 10 m and 30 m on 2 March 2023, a cloud-free scene with a stage of 7.250 m at WLd. Light-blue pixels are bar reclassified as water at 30 m, and yellow pixels are water reclassified as bar. Reclassified pixels lie along sand-water boundaries, consistent with mixed pixels. The test isolates spatial resolution and does not quantify other differences between Landsat and Sentinel-2. Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. S1).","paragraph":758,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_03.png","width":2047,"height":1642,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_03.png","sha256":"8ea9140c2f97dc3242128af6440ec25348f5184dc255ae6b600924d48d81c98d"}]},{"id":"6.4","chapter":6,"title":"Morphological change and active mechanized sand extraction in the mined reach","caption":"Figure 6.4. Morphological change and active mechanized sand extraction in the mined reach. (a) Maxar image (0.5 m) from 2010, before extensive mechanized extraction, showing a braided channel with broad exposed bars, distinct bar margins and several well-defined flow channels. (b) The same reach in a WorldView-2 image (0.3 m) from 2024, with the former bar-and-channel morphology disrupted by fragmented bar surfaces, irregular excavation scars, numerous water-filled pits and poorly defined flow paths. (c) Oblique aerial view, January 2024, showing mechanized dredging installations and pipelines across the exposed dry-season bar and the adjacent low-flow channel. (d) An active extraction installation, with its elevated support structure, pumping equipment, discharge pipe and piles of extracted sand. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 3).","paragraph":775,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_04.jpeg","width":926,"height":1210,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_04.jpeg","sha256":"613a694e46c1a8cd2fff5d4d7d48441f03a50dac49ec0a8ff6ccfc080ec479ed"}]},{"id":"6.5","chapter":6,"title":"Dry-season exposed bar area and river stage, 1988 to 2025","caption":"Figure 6.5. Dry-season exposed bar area and river stage, 1988 to 2025. (a) Bar area and dry-season water level at WLd through time, with the three mining periods. (b) Bar area against water level at WLd for the pre-mining, early mining and mechanized periods. (c) Signed bar-area anomaly, observed minus expected bar area from the pre-mining regression. Negative values indicate less exposed bar than expected. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 4).","paragraph":780,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_05.png","width":2046,"height":1699,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_05.png","sha256":"9c524457f62332fa0168209cfe26403cee0252ab9369d12032256997396dd17b"}]},{"id":"6.6","chapter":6,"title":"Repeat channel cross-sections and thalweg elevation, 2008 to 2024","caption":"Figure 6.6. Repeat channel cross-sections and thalweg elevation, 2008 to 2024. (a) to (d) Surveys at the upstream section XS1 and the mined sections XS2 to XS4. XS3 lies beside the downstream gauge WLd, and XS4 lies c. 5 km below it. Triangles mark the principal thalwegs. (e) Thalweg elevation through time at the four sections. Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 5).","paragraph":785,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_06.png","width":2007,"height":2441,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_06.png","sha256":"6dcb4c6607e8d6247a520e0932f95afed7cbbc77b143423ee24507776fb17af8"}]},{"id":"6.7","chapter":6,"title":"Dry-season water level, annual stage range and water-surface slope, 1988 to 2025","caption":"Figure 6.7. Dry-season water level, annual stage range and water-surface slope, 1988 to 2025. (a) Daily water level at the upstream gauge WLu and the downstream gauge WLd. (b) Dry-season (February to April) mean water level at both gauges and the annual range of daily water level at each (right axis). The WLu range for 1999 is omitted because of the gauge gap. (c) Dry-season water-surface slope between the gauges. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 6).","paragraph":791,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_07.png","width":2007,"height":1658,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_07.png","sha256":"080524ce1ef2927bda02830e7a696d30d6da580c64a89bd8e6927a514384b794"}]},{"id":"6.8","chapter":6,"title":"Near-river groundwater level at GWn, 1988 to 2024","caption":"Figure 6.8. Near-river groundwater level at GWn, 1988 to 2024. (a) Weekly water-table depth (m below ground), with dry-season (November to April) and monsoon (May to October) means and trends fitted before and after 2013. (b) Yearly dry-season water-table depth at GWn against yearly dry-season water level at WLd, by mining period. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 7).","paragraph":796,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_08.png","width":2046,"height":1826,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_08.png","sha256":"747c399ca61e4284bc6d698ad893a83614dea2cca0c48c981ac6505834873725"}]},{"id":"6.9","chapter":6,"title":"Overbank flooding and floodplain inundation from gauge records and radar, 1996 to 2025","caption":"Figure 6.9. Overbank flooding and floodplain inundation from gauge records and radar, 1996 to 2025. (a) Mean monsoon stage at WLd against mean monsoon discharge at WLu, before and during mining. (b) Days per monsoon with stage at or above 12 m and 13 m, by period. (c, d) Sentinel-1 inundation extent on two monsoon dates of the mechanized period with upstream discharge within 4%: (c) 12 August 2017 and (d) 23 September 2025. Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 8).","paragraph":802,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-9-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-9-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_09.png","width":937,"height":1082,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_09.png","sha256":"8d2c90ff680ec90894f1b0bd0e71b946d7fbb953b1a66a4c309d511d5150ba69"}]},{"id":"6.10","chapter":6,"title":"Annual rainfall and dry-season river stage, 1988 to 2025","caption":"Figure 6.10. Annual rainfall and dry-season river stage, 1988 to 2025. (a) Annual rainfall at RF1 before and during mining, with the trend within each period. (b) Dry-season stage at WLd against annual rainfall before and during mining, with the pre-mining mean and minimum and two wet mining-period years labeled. (c) Observed dry-season stage at WLd minus the stage expected from rainfall under the pre-mining relation. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 9).","paragraph":809,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-10-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-10-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_10.png","width":2045,"height":915,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_10.png","sha256":"5aa40cb254687e40127c18e02a01089453c1c40c91e78b999fd4b28ee05a27bc"}]},{"id":"6.11","chapter":6,"title":"Discharge and stage-discharge ratings, 1996 to 2025","caption":"Figure 6.11. Discharge and stage-discharge ratings, 1996 to 2025. (a) Annual mean discharge at WLu, with the five-year mean and trend. (b) Stage-discharge ratings at WLu and WLd before and during mining. (c) Fall in stage at a fixed discharge between the pre-mining and mining periods at both gauges, across a range of discharges. (d) Stage anomaly relative to the pre-mining ratings through time at both gauges. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 10).","paragraph":815,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-11-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-11-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_11.png","width":2046,"height":1508,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_11.png","sha256":"d60255c08fbb7102114c62ef033786f4c1ae05492c81efeb53892fe8f3e211e5"}]},{"id":"6.12","chapter":6,"title":"Groundwater levels at the near-river and reference wells","caption":"Figure 6.12. Groundwater levels at the near-river and reference wells. (a) Annual mean water-table depth at GWn and GWref. (b) Excess deepening of groundwater level at GWn relative to its pre-mining relation with GWref. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 11).","paragraph":820,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-6-12-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-6-12-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch6_12.png","width":2047,"height":1826,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch6_12.png","sha256":"9b6bf4f3e7a326f93b421e563da5a985e7f76fe060b831f3f17bae4b0f475c27"}]},{"id":"7.1","chapter":7,"title":"The study reaches, monitoring stations, cross-sections and record periods of the four rivers","caption":"Figure 7.1. The study reaches, monitoring stations, cross-sections and record periods of the four rivers. The panels show: (a) the front of the Shillong Plateau from the Jadukata River to the Lubha River, with the location of the study area in Bangladesh; (b) the Piyain main and spill channels downstream of Jaflong and the Sari-Gowain reach from Lalakhal to Gowainghat; (c) the Laurergarh fan and the Jadukata–Rakti reach to 21 km downstream; (d) the Lubhachorra reach; (e to g) larger-scale imagery of the Jaflong bends, the Laurergarh fan and Lubhachorra, and (h), on the following page, the periods of the gauge records, the survey years and the temporal coverage of satellite images. Blue filled and open circles mark gauges with and without discharge records, and purple diamonds mark BWDB sections. Filled and open triangles distinguish the regional wells from the near-river wells set aside in Appendix 7A, and red crosses mark rain gauges. SY060 lies outside panel (b), 21 km south of Sharighat. Dashed outlines mark channel belts. The channels follow OpenStreetMap centerlines through the stations, within the maximum water extent of the Joint Research Centre (JRC) Global Surface Water dataset (Pekel et al., 2016). Dry-season Landsat and Sentinel-2 images measure channel-belt area, and monsoon Sentinel-1 images measure inundation. Imagery: Esri World Imagery; basemap: Esri Light Gray Canvas and Shaded Relief; stations: BWDB; section coordinates: BWDB hydrology portal, 3 November 2026.\n\nFigure 7.1 (continued). (h) The periods of the gauge records, the years of the cross-section surveys and the dates of the satellite images analyzed for the four rivers. Blue bars mark gauge records of water level and discharge, and gray bars mark records of water level only. Purple diamonds mark the survey years. Ticks mark the dates of the dry-season Landsat and Sentinel-2 images and of the monsoon Sentinel-1 images, with the number of images in brackets. Data are from the BWDB, Landsat Collection 2, Sentinel-2 Level-2A and Sentinel-1.","paragraph":868,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_01.png","width":2160,"height":2770,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_01.png","sha256":"ca6e4d94b3612839815a99ac2a1dc8d991ec4530ce1c7d1a5ec27f23c168b847"},{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-1-2.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-1-2.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_02.png","width":2700,"height":1350,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_02.png","sha256":"472c766bd981c71b8ab4d6161e6ac9bb25a9b8b084528e542fb24d3177c00598"}]},{"id":"7.2","chapter":7,"title":"The design of the analysis is shown as a schematic, without data","caption":"Figure 7.2. The design of the analysis is shown as a schematic, without data. For each link of the impact chain of Chapter 6, the diagram gives the records available on the four rivers, the measure computed from them, with its section in brackets, and a sketch of the signature expected from bed lowering by extraction. Bed lowering is drawn in orange, and the signatures of the alternative explanations are drawn as gray dashed lines. The row for river stage includes sketches of the five gauge tests of Table 7A.2.","paragraph":874,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_03.png","width":2160,"height":1665,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_03.png","sha256":"2eaa004cc0c48b70e5f3bb06ad519f69aca80c9a91022896f361b093c5b5eb52"}]},{"id":"7.3","chapter":7,"title":"Dry-season areas of exposed bar and water in the channel belt downstream of each gauge are shown for 1988 to 2026","caption":"Figure 7.3. Dry-season areas of exposed bar and water in the channel belt downstream of each gauge are shown for 1988 to 2026. The method is that of Figure 6.5, applied to (a to c) the Jadukata River downstream of the Laurergarh gauge, (d to f) the Piyain spill channel downstream of the Jaflong Spill gauge, (g to i) the Sari-Gowain River downstream of the Sharighat gauge and (j to l) the Lubha River downstream of the Lubhachorra gauge. The left panels show season-year medians of Landsat bar and water area and of gauge stage on image dates, where a season year is the dry season from December to April named by the year in which it ends. Sentinel-2 bar medians are added as a check (open circles). The middle panels show bar area against stage, colored by period (blue, light blue, orange and red for 1988 to 1999, 2000 to 2011, 2012 to 2016 and 2017 to 2026), with the baseline regression (solid line) and its extrapolation (dotted line). The right panels show observed minus expected bar area for each image (gray dots) and as season-year medians (colored bars). Dotted vertical lines separate periods. Data are from Landsat 5, 7, 8 and 9 Collection 2 and Sentinel-2 Level-2A, processed in Google Earth Engine, and recorded BWDB water levels.","paragraph":911,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_04.png","width":2280,"height":2880,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_04.png","sha256":"c4c9ef7ee6f5f79ac6aab11f8e8c368101ffdce899e6be8a04f5035d3665d859"}]},{"id":"7.4","chapter":7,"title":"The surveyed change in bed elevation is shown at the BWDB cross-sections of the Piyain, Sari-Gowain and Jadukata rivers","caption":"Figure 7.4. The surveyed change in bed elevation is shown at the BWDB cross-sections of the Piyain, Sari-Gowain and Jadukata rivers. (a) to (d) Four representative repeat cross-sections are shown, with earlier surveys in gray, the next-to-last survey in orange and the last survey in red, and with triangles at the thalwegs. The blue lines in (a) are the dry-season levels at the Jaflong Spill gauge in 2012 (dashed) and 2023 (dotted). (e) to (h) The change between the first and the last survey is shown at every usable section against its straight-line distance from the gauge, for the thalweg (filled triangles) and for the stage at equal low-flow area (open circles). Section names are shortened (PYN2 is RMPYN2), and the numbers in (h) are RMJDR7 to RMJDR1. The thalweg of RMJDR3 is not shown because of the spike in its 2012 profile. (i) to (l) The change at the section nearest each gauge is compared through time with the annual stage at the reference discharge at that gauge (blue, with a band of one standard error), relative to the first survey year. Gray lines show the thalwegs of the other sections. Table 7A.1 gives the values, and Figures 7A.6 and 7A.7 show all profiles. Data are from the BWDB.","paragraph":919,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_05.png","width":2160,"height":2790,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_05.png","sha256":"b37c8ee4b7ecd7c9c15455580a11526f386443b45752ccc907e819dd6cb9e7db"}]},{"id":"7.5","chapter":7,"title":"Drivers and responses on the Jadukata River are compared for 1990 to 2026","caption":"Figure 7.5. Drivers and responses on the Jadukata River are compared for 1990 to 2026. (a) Monthly standardized anomalies are shown for rainfall (12-month totals), discharge, river water level and depth to groundwater, with the sign of the depth reversed, so that negative values mean a deeper water table. Blue is above normal for that calendar month and red is below, and the line is the 12-month running mean. (b) The trend per decade is shown with its 95% confidence interval, for all months (circles) and for December to March (diamonds). Hollow markers show trends that are not significant. (c) Decade means of the standardized index are shown for the drivers (gray) and the responses (black). (d) Water level is plotted against discharge by decade (1990s blue, 2000s light blue, 2010s orange, 2020 to 2026 red) at the Laurergarh gauge (SW131.5). The arrow marks the change in water level at the median discharge of December to March. Data are from the BWDB.","paragraph":926,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_06.png","width":2160,"height":1890,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_06.png","sha256":"c2ef90f7793b373e89bb385a119cfd693857b73ea6049bb343f583b78863855d"}]},{"id":"7.6","chapter":7,"title":"Drivers and responses on the Piyain River are compared for 1990 to 2026","caption":"Figure 7.6. Drivers and responses on the Piyain River are compared for 1990 to 2026. The panels are as in Figure 7.5. Panel (d) shows the Ratnar bhanga gauge (SW233) on the main channel and the Jaflong Spill gauge (SW233A) on the spill channel. Data are from the BWDB.","paragraph":928,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_07.png","width":2160,"height":1998,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_07.png","sha256":"eff4b687997086722f2a4c4acee2d7bba31bfec86f1adf6d405a78617057b46d"}]},{"id":"7.7","chapter":7,"title":"Drivers and responses on the Sari-Gowain River are compared for 1990 to 2026","caption":"Figure 7.7. Drivers and responses on the Sari-Gowain River are compared for 1990 to 2026. The panels are as in Figure 7.5, and panel (d) shows the Sharighat gauge (SW251). Data are from the BWDB.","paragraph":930,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_08.png","width":2160,"height":1998,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_08.png","sha256":"72491f6f119671c51db2fbee01b95df108530d5f19d29ce8e877361d7deb9ee7"}]},{"id":"7.8","chapter":7,"title":"Drivers and responses on the Lubha River are compared for 1990 to 2026","caption":"Figure 7.8. Drivers and responses on the Lubha River are compared for 1990 to 2026. The panels are as in Figure 7.5. The groundwater row shows the Kanaighat reference well (SY087), and panel (d) shows the Lubhachorra gauge (SW326), where discharge has been measured since August 2009. Data are from the BWDB.","paragraph":932,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_09.png","width":2160,"height":1890,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_09.png","sha256":"d1375c27264e022041c606ee6416474b51ee3d6586255c81a2898fdd2afa07a6"}]},{"id":"7.9","chapter":7,"title":"Floodplain inundation in 2017 and 2025 compared for the four rivers, as for the Someshwari River (Figure 6.9)","caption":"Figure 7.9. Floodplain inundation in 2017 and 2025 compared for the four rivers, as for the Someshwari River (Figure 6.9). (a) to (h) Water detected by Sentinel-1, where the VV backscatter is below −16 dB, as present on the 2017 image (blue) and the 2025 image (orange) that are closest in discharge, with the discharge at the named gauge, the rainfall of the preceding 15 days and the inundated area in the floodplain zone (zone) and in the short-reach zone (reach). Light blue marks the floodplain zone and darker blue the short-reach zone. The dashed line marks the 2 km band around the short reach, and the triangle marks the gauge location. (i) Change in inundated area from 2017 to 2025 shown for all monsoon images of the two years, at the same discharge and 15-day rainfall, with 95% confidence intervals. The dashed line marks the single pair of images of the Someshwari River (Section 6.4.6). (j) Change in inundated area between 2015 to 2020 and 2021 to 2025, the years after the extraction ban on the Lubha River, with intervals obtained from resampling whole years. Basemap: Esri World Topographic Map.","paragraph":954,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-9-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-9-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_10.png","width":2160,"height":3253,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_10.png","sha256":"9ed5de430fa40c1bcb4f828d2abf4d1e8dd6d01387f4550a22e65f70ccd54655"}]},{"id":"7.10","chapter":7,"title":"The river stage of the Lubha River during and after extraction as recorded at the Lubhachorra gauge (SW326)","caption":"Figure 7.10. The river stage of the Lubha River during and after extraction as recorded at the Lubhachorra gauge (SW326). (a) Water level plotted against discharge for the measurements of December to March at discharges of up to 20 m³ s⁻¹, colored by phase of extraction. The lines have the common slope of the fit and the mean level of 2016 to 2020, 2021 to 2022 and 2023 to 2026, with the dotted vertical line marking the reference discharge of 6.9 m³ s⁻¹. (b) The river stage at 6.9 m³ s⁻¹ for each water year, with one standard error of the fit, and the mean within each phase of sediment extraction (horizontal lines and numbers). Shading marks manual extraction (blue) and mechanized extraction (orange), and the dashed line marks the ban of February 2021. (c) The low-season water level, the 10th percentile of January to March, shown with the median of the discharges measured from December to March. (d) The median areas of exposed bar and water in the channel belt downstream of the gauge, detected from Landsat images taken at stages of 4.3 to 4.9 m, with the number of images, and the median bar area in the Sentinel-2 images at the same stages (open circles). Data are from the BWDB, Landsat Collection 2 and Sentinel-2 Level-2A.","paragraph":965,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-7-10-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-7-10-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch7_11.png","width":2220,"height":1890,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch7_11.png","sha256":"0a9e49fda5082d6e44f180d706579fd239c39989fcf1ed64ce8c13464e62cbb0"}]},{"id":"8.1","chapter":8,"title":"Conventions of the causal loop diagrams used in this chapter","caption":"Figure 8.1. Conventions of the causal loop diagrams used in this chapter. (a) Link polarity, all else equal; a sign states direction only. (b) Support and delay, shown independently of polarity: solid links are supported by triangulated evidence, dashed links are retained as inferences, and double bars mark delays only where they are evidenced. (c) Reinforcing (R) and balancing (B) loops, distinguished by the number of negative links they contain. (d) The evidence record kept for each link (Table 8.4).","paragraph":1009,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_01.png","width":2100,"height":1380,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_01.png","sha256":"ef8fa8ddfdf8cdf61000b83c1c42d8edc4ead6a52c62c6df9e1c863bdf80393e"}]},{"id":"8.2","chapter":8,"title":"Analytical approach and study design","caption":"Figure 8.2. Analytical approach and study design. Survey, discussion and interview, and documentary evidence (1) is analyzed for distribution across occupational positions and for mechanism and direction (2). Political ecology specifies what is modeled, and qualitative system dynamics represents this as variables and signed links in a causal loop diagram of 13 variables and 22 links, each traceable to its evidence (3). The result is three feedback loops, occupational positions mapped to those loops, and a diagnosis of the trap (4). Unsupported links remain open for further field evidence. Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 1).","paragraph":1017,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_02.png","width":2280,"height":1338,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_02.png","sha256":"5309d1b05196457d745c9d320610f30ae551fe2de27d743f132977dd954e4fe1"}]},{"id":"8.3","chapter":8,"title":"Response geometry across occupational positions","caption":"Figure 8.3. Response geometry across occupational positions. Dots show group percentages for household items (valid responses; denominators in Table 8.2) and governance and temporal module items (full group sizes; Table 8.3). From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 3).","paragraph":1045,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_03.png","width":3180,"height":2370,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_03.png","sha256":"73ef775ce898a630640a491f28e4b0a8e4cda0f37edd4b788c7442ec3c840921"}]},{"id":"8.4","chapter":8,"title":"Revenue allocation per truckload of sand from the leaseholder’s constant-sum exercise","caption":"Figure 8.4. Revenue allocation per truckload of sand from the leaseholder’s constant-sum exercise. Of every 100 BDT paid by the buyer, 54 BDT was allocated to the leaseholder syndicate and machine owner and 11 BDT to laborers at the ghat. Unofficial payments (6 BDT) exceeded official royalties (4 BDT). From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 4).","paragraph":1048,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_04.png","width":3060,"height":1170,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_04.png","sha256":"6fc7d1b896738720fe122687ee91007ef1bc6ab354782a4db6a0529f8c0e6537"}]},{"id":"8.5","chapter":8,"title":"Recalled five-year change (full group sizes)","caption":"Figure 8.5. Recalled five-year change (full group sizes). Recognition of environmental decline is broadly shared; job change divides by group, with 82 and 73% of the mining-linked groups reporting more jobs against 12% of Non-Related respondents. From Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 5).","paragraph":1056,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_05.png","width":3150,"height":1498,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_05.png","sha256":"df4aa1ea4ace5d49ea8b9b0c2de7889111f465420118ffa0ce3d74c4f27dd6d0"}]},{"id":"8.6","chapter":8,"title":"The corrective pathway","caption":"Figure 8.6. The corrective pathway. (a) Perceived enforcement (S3) and fairness (S5); labels denote the percentage in each response category. (b) Complaint experience (S4): 97 of 337 respondents attempted a formal complaint, of whom 5 reported the issue addressed (5.2%), and 31 of the 42 who reported facing pressure to desist were in the Non-Related group. Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 6).","paragraph":1070,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-6-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-6-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_06.png","width":3060,"height":3640,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_06.png","sha256":"a86ee205f61ec42d021d1b2ca2793ea566cf1474ddff35b2f3c9046287fe655c"}]},{"id":"8.7","chapter":8,"title":"Feedback structure of persistent sand extraction","caption":"Figure 8.7. Feedback structure of persistent sand extraction. (a) Causal loop diagram of the thirteen variables shown in Table 8.4. Solid arrows are links supported by triangulated evidence, and the dashed arrow (benefit concentration to control over extraction assets) is inferred. Link color and sign give polarity (blue positive, red negative), circled markers identify the loops, shaded boxes mark the six power-related variables, and double bars mark the two evidenced delays. R1 (livelihood reinforcement) and R2 (accumulation and protection) are reinforcing; B1, the corrective pathway, is balancing and is entered by two negative links from political protection. (b) Occupational positions mapped to loop segments, with each group’s modal attribution of livelihood change (S8) as edge labels: sand-mining income (Core SM), transport demand (Linked-to-SM), and riverbank erosion (Non-Related). Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 7).","paragraph":1084,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-7-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-7-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_07.png","width":2340,"height":2520,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_07.png","sha256":"42550eca8e3a1a8947f72ff6a28d1cc4ad3fc6352645c6f1361f01571686a8c6"}]},{"id":"8.8","chapter":8,"title":"The study reach within the wider sand corridor","caption":"Figure 8.8. The study reach within the wider sand corridor. District and national political actors (exogenous) act on the reach through lease allocation, political protection, and instructions not to interfere, while sand moves to construction markets in Dhaka and sediment is delivered from Meghalaya. Unofficial payments (6 BDT per 100 BDT paid by the buyer) exceeded the official royalty (4 BDT; Figure 8.4). The dashed boundary is the system boundary of the causal loop diagram (Figure 8.7a), and shaded boxes hold its power-related variables. Values are from Sections 8.4.1 to 8.4.5, and annual extraction is from Islam et al. (2026). Adapted from Chyon et al. (in review, Fig. 8).","paragraph":1111,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-8-8-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-8-8-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch8_08.png","width":1920,"height":1800,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch8_08.png","sha256":"6061f19b0702a3e4b05ad1e19e303f1c467122c6d81958bd59f42b667f28083a"}]},{"id":"9.1","chapter":9,"title":"Findings of the thesis along the DMP-SGM pathway","caption":"Figure 9.1. Findings of the thesis along the DMP-SGM pathway. (a) Driver: the number of extraction sites with a dated onset in each period (Section 4.3.2), divided between the gravel areas, which are sectors S1 and S6 of Table 4.1, and the sand-bed rivers of sectors S2 to S5. (b) Extent: the conservative footprint of the 79 extraction-type sites in each dry season from 1988 to 2025 (Figure 4.6), divided in the same way, with dashed lines and lighter shading marking the national ban on gravel extraction, from February 2020 to the change of national government in August 2024. (c) Impact: the four linked changes measured on the Someshwari River (Chapter 6). (d) Management: the reported outcome of the 97 attempts at a formal complaint in the Someshwari survey, in which each circle is one attempt (Section 8.4.4). The text beneath each panel gives the main result and the outcome for each hypothesis, and the dashed arrow above the stages marks the effect of bans on the extent of extraction.","paragraph":1141,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-9-1-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-9-1-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch9_01.png","width":1950,"height":1425,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch9_01.png","sha256":"d8e412eb30b2bb9063b0c1fec5fc69f958f10eb3dca8238781762b73cc6318f9"}]},{"id":"9.2","chapter":9,"title":"The six study rivers compared","caption":"Figure 9.2. The six study rivers compared. Rows are grouped by bed material, and the chapters that examine each river are given beneath its name. The timeline shows the phases of extraction from 1995 to 2026 (Table 4.4; Table 7.2), in which lighter bars mark manual or early extraction, darker bars mark mechanized extraction, diagonally hatched bars mark a ban in force, and cross-hatched bars mark extraction that resumed or continued without authorization. Gray lines mark the years before the first dated extraction, and dashed outlines mark extraction by boats and pumps whose dates are not known. Within each group, the rivers are ordered from east to west. The dashed red line marks the change of national government on 5 August 2024. The bars to the right give the fall in stage at the same low discharge at each gauge (Section 6.4.8; Table 7.3). The matrix records whether each link of the impact chain was established, indicated, evaluated with the change not established, or not evaluated, and whether residents were surveyed.","paragraph":1163,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-9-2-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-9-2-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch9_02.png","width":1950,"height":2241,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch9_02.png","sha256":"fff10be7bf94a9b6acebc8d696fa6ce9107e4f7d4bccf72a989efe11f680c508"}]},{"id":"9.3","chapter":9,"title":"Responses to bans on extraction, and the social cost of the ban on the Lubha River","caption":"Figure 9.3. Responses to bans on extraction, and the social cost of the ban on the Lubha River. (a) Area of pits in the active channel and in the abandoned channels and floodplain of the Lubha River at Lubhachorra from 2012 to 2024, with the period of the ban shaded (data from Chyon et al., 2026). (b) Responses of 55 residents of Lubhachorra surveyed in February 2024 (Table 5.2). (c) Dry-season stage of the Piyain River at the Jaflong Spill gauge, and the stage at the reference discharge of 10.9 m³ s⁻¹ in each water year (Section 7.4.3), with the national ban of February 2020 to August 2024 shaded. Bars give one standard error where it exceeds 0.1 m. The two measures agree to 2024 and differ in 2025 and 2026, when the stage at the reference discharge is uncertain. (d) Dry-season stage of the Someshwari River at the Durgapur gauge, where no ban was enforced. In (c) and (d), the dry-season stage is the 10th percentile of the daily stage from January to March of each year, calculated from records of the Bangladesh Water Development Board and given relative to the Public Works Datum (PWD). The two panels have the same vertical span.","paragraph":1174,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-9-3-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-9-3-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch9_03.png","width":1950,"height":1590,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch9_03.png","sha256":"dc9872096e3ea40e79b0532791f083ffbd506f133c47b20e9deaa6912a140047"}]},{"id":"9.4","chapter":9,"title":"Scale mismatch and political protection","caption":"Figure 9.4. Scale mismatch and political protection. (a) The findings of Chapters 4 to 8, placed by the distance and time over which each process or decision operated. Both axes are logarithmic, and the positions are orders of magnitude taken from the chapters cited in the key, not measured values. Processes that were still continuing are placed at their duration to the end of their record. The arrow above point 6 marks infill that was still incomplete. The shape and color of each symbol indicate its stage in the DMP-SGM pathway. The two shaded fields mark the decisions made within the lease system and the lowering of the bed, the stage and the water table, and the dashed arrow marks the mismatch in scale between them. Open symbols mark the decisions imposed from outside the lease system (points 11 and 13). (b) The corrective pathway on the Someshwari River, from the recognition of harm to the limitation of extraction, with the results of the household survey at each step (Section 8.4.4). Political protection, which was funded by the concentrated revenue of extraction, weakened the pathway at the two links marked in red (Section 8.5.2). Lighter boxes and dashed arrows mark the steps that were rarely or never completed.","paragraph":1180,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-9-4-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-9-4-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch9_04.png","width":1950,"height":2310,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch9_04.png","sha256":"9216feafdeb4fed15f8fbc92dba2fc28cbd1552097e54805b212c03ec565151b"}]},{"id":"9.5","chapter":9,"title":"The combination of evidence in the thesis","caption":"Figure 9.5. The combination of evidence in the thesis. The three types of evidence were aligned by reach, by dry season and by dated event, and tested against rival explanations in a fixed order. Their combination had three outcomes, each shown with an example from Chapters 4 to 8. The tags name the records combined in each example, with their color denoting the type of evidence, and the gray tag marking evidence of more than one type.","paragraph":1187,"parts":[{"src":"assets/figures/fig-9-5-1.webp","thumb":"assets/thumbs/fig-9-5-1.webp","original":"assets/originals/ch9_05.png","width":1950,"height":1320,"origin":"Separate figure file","sourceFile":"ch9_05.png","sha256":"a8bfb96721c0bebf52612f554d906d8af5c3dc8e87b6ff71111b779622055bfc"}]}];
